hey everyone this is kevin from  thechestwebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the matanovsky  gambit and this is a variation in the  owens defense  from black standpoint i actually don't  recommend playing it from white  standpoint you actually could run into  it  every now and then and so this video is  going to be more about an introduction  into the gambit  and how from white standpoint they  should optimally  play against it because it looks  extremely tricky  and many times if you don't know how to  play it you may fall into some of their  trap so it starts out with  e4 pretty common and then b6 this is the  owens defense  most times when players don't want to  counter attack in the center of the  board it's  usually correct just to control the  center yourself so e4  and then d4 bishop here to b7 a hyper  modern defense trying to control the  center with their minor  pieces and then bishop here to d3 you  could also play knight to c3 to protect  this light square that's being attacked  by  the bishop but many times players are  looking to play bishop here to d3 get  their knight involved into the game  castle on the king side get your king  safety ass  soon as possible and this is where the  madanovsky gambit comes into play  and it's with the move f5 and it looks  extremely tricky not something you would  expect to see too often and really the  thought behind this  is they want you to take here on f5 and  then they're going to play bishop takes  here on g2  attacking the rook here on h1  now unlike a lot of gambits where you  may not want to  take there's usually a better line in  this particular game but actually  recommend  taking that pawn here on f5 letting them  bring the bishop here to g2  attacking the rook if white plays  optimally which hopefully after this  video you will be able to  white will definitely be ahead so from  here  the best move is for white to play queen  to h5 the only legal option that black  has is to play pawn to g6 and then  white's going to capture here on  g6 now black pretty much has to play  bishop to  g7 here if they try something tricky  knight to  let's say f6 attacking the queen okay  well now we could just take with our  pawn here on  h7 this is check the only legal move  they have is to take our queen  but that's going to be checkmate with  bishop here to  g6 the king is just in a terrible spot  he is trapped  nowhere to go so that's not gonna work  can't bring the knight  here to f6 they do in fact need to bring  their bishop here  to g7 as soon as they do we're going to  take here on h7  and then from here the king is going to  come to f8 this  is also the only legal move that black  has so a lot of these are  forced there's not a lot of options that  black has  in this position this is where white can  play  sub-optimally but you'll definitely  understand how to play  correctly now white really should be  playing knight to f3 in this spot but  there's some other  tempting variations that you could look  at one of those might be hey  let's go ahead and take this knight here  on g8  this is a free piece hey my pawn is  going to be taken at some point  anyway so we might as well just go ahead  and take with our pawn  we can promote to a queen or a rook it  doesn't really matter we're forcing our  opponent  to go ahead and take material right here  but then the problem is  if you look at this position black's  just going to take this  rook here on h1 and so uh  they're also attacking with their rook  the queen here on h5  and so we have to retreat our queen back  and they play bishop takes here on h1  and then maybe knight to c3 how else do  you really attack here  pawn to e6 to prepare to get the queen  involved into the game this  also stops the knight coming down here  to d5  very important maybe bishop here to g5  attacking our queen but queen here to e8  and yes the king does not have any pawns  in front of it  but also black has a fine setup and i  i think uh black's gonna be fine in  this game so if we come back that's one  variation you could look at another one  might be okay let's just keep attacking  with the queen queen to  f5 may make sense  uh if you kind of look at it but then  you start to think okay well what is  black have here knight 2  f6 this stops the threat it also  prepares for rook taking here on h7 and  then you realize this is not really a  a very good move the only option you may  have you know bishop here 2h6  but then just rook takes here on h7 and  it's very  tough to see how white's going to  continue they're also going to lose this  rook here on  h1 so the best move in this position is  for white to play knight to f3  and this is where black has to play  correctly just to somewhat stay  in the game white's definitely going to  be ahead in  this game but if they look at this and  say okay my plan all along was to take  this rook here on h1  that is going to prove fatal for them  because  now we can just play the move knight to  e5 this is threatening the move  queen to f7 checkmate the only way to  stop this  is to play the move queen to ea any  other option they have  it's a pretty quick forced mate but if  they play queen  to h8 still very difficult for them to  hold on to things  we play pawn takes here on g8  check they pretty much have to take with  their rook here don't want to take with  the king  you can see that's going to be fatal  after the queen takes the queen here on  e8 um so pretty much have to take with  their rook here  but then after queen 2 f5 the only  option is bishop to  f6 and then bishop two h6 you could see  an exchange here  and then after the king takes now queen  down to h7 you can see the bishop on  d3 starting to play into the action here  defending the  queen so forcing the king to move and  now  bishop to g6 and this is going to be  lights out for black doesn't really  matter how they continue  sure they could take material and yes we  can take their queen automatically but  we don't have to because  we could take take with our pawn here on  e5 and you can see the queen is trapped  the queen can't go anywhere um or it is  going to fall and if it moves away  well then white has the threat of queen  to f7  checkmate so pretty much has to take  material and you can see  that is not going to end well so after  we play that knight to f3  they can't really take with their bishop  here on h1 they really need to be  playing the move  knight to f6 this is attacking our queen  so we definitely are going to move our  queen we're going to move it to  g6 now from here they have a couple  options same thing as before it would be  a mistake  if they take here on h1 but we are going  to play something slightly different  than moving our knights  instead we're going to play bishop down  here to h6 and this is going to be  lights out no real way for them to stop  this  you know they could take with their  bishop right here queen takes this is  check  king 2 f7 knight up here to  g5 lots of variations uh on how we could  finish this but after king 2e8 bishop to  g6 and this is going to be checkmate so  they can't really  take with their bishop here on h1 after  queen  to g6 you can see their their threat  that they wanted all along  in this game it was to play bishop to g2  and then take here on h1 and we are  putting them in so many situations where  anytime they do that it would be a  mistake and they would lose the game and  that's the whole point of  playing as white in this is knowing that  yes  i will go ahead and allow you to  continue down  your threat in this gambit but i'm never  actually going to allow you to take  the material here on h1 so after queen 2  g6 they really need to play the move  bishop takes here  on f3 that knight could be extremely  dangerous they just don't need to deal  with it so after that  then rook to g1 the rook is now safe  don't have to worry about the bishop  taking any more they could take with  their rook here this rook on h7 is  protected by the knight here on f6  but then the queen just comes back here  to g3 and this does a couple things once  a discovered attack the bishop here on  d3 is now attacking the rook here on h7  and also the queen now attacks this  bishop here on f3  so that is the madonovsky gambit  hopefully you enjoyed this video again  if you  see that from your opponent playing the  black pieces it's okay  you don't have to worry if they actually  play the move  pawn to f5 this is a mistake  on their part you can gladly accept it  let them take here on  g2 and then you can start the attack  with queen to h5  knowing that you're actually never going  to give up that rook here on h1 so thank  you guys so much for watching the video  if you enjoyed it if you wouldn't mind  hitting that like button  let me know if there is another video  you want me to make a video on  you can let me know in the comments  below or you can always email me as well  at the chess website  gmail.com but thanks again i'll see you  guys in the next video  you

